Here are my findings. This is the Spring PetClinic "Evolve" codebase, where `POST /api/owners` is implemented as an OfficeFloor pipeline (a chain of small Java functions defined in a YAML flow). The owner DTOs (`OwnerDto`, `OwnerFieldsDto`) are code-generated at build time from `openapi.yml` into package `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto` — there are no hand-written `.java` files for them in the tree.

## 1. POST owners YAML flow

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`

This defines the create-owner pipeline. Each step names a Java function class and its `next` step. The capacity check is the `capacity` step, wired after `identity` and before `dailyLimit`:

```yaml
identity:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.RequireUniqueIdentity
  govern: [ transaction ]
  next: capacity

capacity:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.RequireCityCapacity
  govern: [ transaction ]
  next: dailyLimit
```

Full flow order: `idempotency -> validate -> registrationDate -> postcode -> email -> identity -> capacity -> dailyLimit -> build -> customerCode -> deduplicateCustomerCode -> namesakes -> possibleDuplicate -> save -> record -> respond`. The terminal `respond` step is `RespondWithOwnerCreated`. (Full contents of the YAML were read; the two blocks above are the load-bearing part.)

## 2. Capacity / city-counting function

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/RequireCityCapacity.java`

This is the hard 50-per-city limit. It counts existing owners with an exactly-matching city and throws when the count is at or above 50:

```java
public class RequireCityCapacity {

    static final int CAPACITY = 50;

    public void service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto request, OwnerRepository ownerRepository)
            throws CityAtCapacityException {
        String city = request.getCity();
        long count = ownerRepository.findAll().stream()
            .filter(existing -> city.equals(existing.getCity()))
            .count();
        if (count >= CAPACITY) {
            throw new CityAtCapacityException(city);
        }
    }
}
```

Note: it counts `ownerRepository.findAll()` unfiltered — it does not exclude soft-deleted owners (there is a `deleted` flag on owners elsewhere in the model).

A separate related per-day limit function exists at `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/RequireDailyCapacity.java` (the `dailyLimit` step), if per-day capacity is also relevant.

## 3. Response DTO for owner responses

The response object is `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto.OwnerDto`, used by the terminal step `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/RespondWithOwnerCreated.java`:

```java
public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerMapper ownerMapper, OwnerRepository ownerRepository,
        ObjectResponse<ResponseEntity<OwnerDto>> response) {
    OwnerDto dto = ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner);
    dto.setMembershipLevel(MembershipCap.level(owner, dto.getMembershipLevel(), ownerRepository));
    dto.setBulkSignupWarning(BulkSignup.warningToday(ownerRepository));
    response.send(ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/api/owners/" + owner.getId())).body(dto));
}
```

`OwnerDto` / `OwnerFields` are generated from the schema in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml`:
- `OwnerFields` schema at lines 1991-2086 (editable input fields: firstName, lastName, address/addressLine1/2, city, telephone, email, postcode, registrationDate, birthDate, sharesHousehold; required: firstName, lastName, city, telephone).
- `Owner` schema at lines 2087+ adds the read-only derived fields including `membershipLevel` (2181), `bulkSignupWarning` (2228), `locality`, `timezone`, `namesakeCount`, `possibleDuplicate`, `deleted`, `selfLink`, `pets`, etc.

Important: there is currently NO `capacityWarning` property in the `openapi.yml` `Owner` schema, and no function sets it. The only reference to `capacityWarning` anywhere in the repo is in the test below.

## 4. Tests for capacity / owner creation

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` — a single test asserting a "approaching capacity" WARNING (not the hard reject):

```java
/** capacity-warning: 'capacityWarning' is true when the owner's city already holds 40-49
 * owners (approaching the 50 hard limit). Fill a fresh city to 40, then the next owner in it warns. */
class AcceptanceTest extends AcceptanceBase {

    @Test
    void coreWarnsWhenApproachingCapacity() throws Exception {
        String city = "Warn" + seq();
        fillCity(city, 40);
        ObjectNode o = ownerNode();
        o.put("city", city);
        int id = createOwnerOk(o);
        getOwner(id).andExpect(jsonPath("$.capacityWarning").value(true));
    }
}
```

This test expects a `capacityWarning` field to be `true` when the city holds 40-49 owners — a field that does not yet exist in the DTO or any function, so this test currently would fail against the code. It exercises `POST /api/owners` then reads back via `GET /api/owners/{id}`.

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` — the black-box test harness. Relevant helpers:
- `fillCity(String city, int n)` (lines 173-179) — creates `n` fully-unique owners all in the same city, used to drive per-city capacity/warning rules.
- `createOwnerOk`, `createOwner`, `getOwner`, `ownerNode()` (a fully-valid unique payload), `uniqueCity()`, etc.
- Class is `@Transactional` so each test rolls back; count/quota rules see only seed data plus what the test creates.

No test currently asserts the 409 hard-rejection at 50 (`CityAtCapacityException`).

## 5. Escalation YAML + handler for capacity rejection

- Exception: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/CityAtCapacityException.java`
  ```java
  public class CityAtCapacityException extends Exception {
      public CityAtCapacityException(String city) {
          super("City at capacity: " + city);
      }
  }
  ```
- Handler: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler.java` — responds HTTP 409 Conflict with an empty body:
  ```java
  public void handle(@Parameter CityAtCapacityException ex,
          ObjectResponse<ResponseEntity<Void>> response) {
      response.send(ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CONFLICT).build());
  }
  ```
- Escalation wiring YAML: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/escalation/org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.CityAtCapacityException.yml`
  ```yaml
  handle:
    class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler
  ```

## Summary of the capacity picture
- Hard limit: 50 owners per city, enforced by `RequireCityCapacity` (`CAPACITY = 50`, rejects at `count >= 50`), which throws `CityAtCapacityException`, mapped to HTTP 409 by the escalation handler + YAML.
- The city count is `ownerRepository.findAll()` filtered by exact `city` string equality (does not exclude soft-deleted owners).
